r the street, so it was turned to 47 degrees, moved about 12 m east and 8 m north-east off the street, and given the extension's depth behind. Stettler's front (about 62 by 18 m): grey-green Bernese sandstone drawn in the nearest pale grey-green, a rusticated base, string course and cornice, two storeys of windows, the low roof, end pavilions a little proud and taller, the tall central pavilion with its arched entrance between engaged columns and its attic. Behind (north-east, toward the Aare): the raw-concrete extension, two levels with a third over its north-west half, a window band, roof lights; paving round, trees on the slope. A path in the ground file crosses the outline; it is not drawn. Still guessed: every height and bay count, the extension's stepping and roof, and which part of the outline is which building."}